{#- media/soulseek uid/gid are pinned (not auto-assigned) on purpose: LSIO
   images take them as PUID/PGID env vars, and slskd gets an explicit
   Podman `User=` — both need a number known at render time, the
   same reasoning as grafana/prometheus's pinned uid/gid. The matching
   formulas/users account entry (pillar/arrstack.sls) must use the same
   numbers.

   qbittorrent.incomplete_dir: local VM disk for torrents still downloading,
   bound to incomplete_container_path (qBittorrent's temp path). Torrents
   write in small random blocks, which the NAS (NFS) only takes at ~1.3 MB/s
   in total — measured 2026-10-18 vs. line speed (~10 MB/s) on local disk.
   qBittorrent moves a finished torrent to /media/torrents in one sequential
   pass, so Sonarr/Radarr still hardlink on the NFS dataset. Sized by the
   VM disk (IaC-opentofu vms/arrstack, disk_size).

   gluetun.uplink_interface: gluetun's own network interface (always eth0 in
   a container). Leak guard: packets carrying the tunnel address as source
   must never leave through it. gluetun's firewall accepts ESTABLISHED
   traffic on any interface, so when tun0 drops, packets of connections
   that were open (FINs, retransmits to peers/trackers, DNS-over-TLS) fell
   back to the default route via eth0 and left the VM unmasqueraded —
   observed 2026-10-18 by taking tun0 down. Added as gluetun post-rules.

   gluetun.cap_add: NET_ADMIN for its firewall/routes/WireGuard link,
   NET_BIND_SERVICE for its DNS server on :53, NET_RAW for its health
   check — all inside its own user/network namespace (Podman creates the
   netns in the container's userns, checked 2026-11-12).
   gluetun.file_puid/file_pgid: owner gluetun gives its /tmp/gluetun files
   (its PUID/PGID, image default 1000). 0 = stays root's: with CHOWN it
   handed forwarded_port to 1000 and then, without DAC_OVERRIDE, couldn't
   rewrite it on the next port-forwarding restart — an endless retry loop
   that re-ran the down/up hooks every few seconds, flipping qBittorrent
   between lo and tun0 (observed 2026-11-13, 374 loops in one boot). Nothing
   else reads the file, so no CHOWN, no DAC_OVERRIDE.
   gluetun.tmpfs: /tmp/gluetun (public IP and forwarded-port files) is
   owned by another user in the image; root without DAC_OVERRIDE can't
   write it, which also kept the port-forwarding hook from running.
   lsio_podman: spec overrides for the LinuxServer images (qbittorrent,
   sonarr, radarr, prowlarr, bazarr): their s6 init starts as root, rewrites
   /etc/passwd for PUID/PGID and chowns /config before dropping to it — so
   writable root fs and the capabilities for that, nothing more.
   Every app keeps the media uid/gid 1:1 in its user namespace: files on
   the NFS share (root_squash) and in config_base stay owned by 970.
   recyclarr: syncs TRaSH Guides quality profiles, custom formats and
   quality definitions into Sonarr/Radarr. Not a long-running container: a
   systemd timer runs `podman run --rm … sync` in gluetun's namespace (so
   the apps are on localhost, like for each other). API keys are read from
   each app's own config.xml at run time and passed as env vars — the apps
   generate them, so there is no second copy (no OpenBao entry) to drift.
   instances: <app>: <instance name>: recyclarr settings minus
   base_url/api_key (added per app). Defaults = TRaSH WEB-1080p (Sonarr)
   and HD Bluray + WEB (Radarr), original language, with the CF groups the
   config-templates enable (Golden Rule HD blocks x265 for HD — also the
   costlier codec to transcode without a GPU, see jellyfin). on_calendar:
   after the 03:00–06:00 enforce window and the 02:00 backup, which stops
   sonarr/radarr. #}
